\title{Coherently Invertible Map}
\taxon{Definition}
\import{macros}
\p{
    A map #{f:A\to B} is \strong{coherently invertible} if it comes equipped
    with a map #{g:B\to A}, homotopies
    ##{
        G:f\circ g\sim\mathsf{id}_B,
        \qquad
        H:g\circ f\sim\mathsf{id}_A,
    }
    and a coherence homotopy
    ##{
        K:\Pi_{x:A}\bigl(G(f(x))=\mathsf{ap}_f(H(x))\bigr).
    }
    The type of such data is
    ##{
        \mathsf{isCohInv}(f)
        \defeq
        \Sigma_{g:B\to A}
        \Sigma_{G:f\circ g\sim\mathsf{id}_B}
        \Sigma_{H:g\circ f\sim\mathsf{id}_A}
        \Pi_{x:A}\bigl(G(f(x))=\mathsf{ap}_f(H(x))\bigr).
    }
    Thus the right inverse law at #{f(x)} agrees with the image under #{f} of
    the left inverse law at #{x}. This is the pointwise, unit-normalized form of
    the usual coherence #{G\mathbin{\cdot}f\sim f\mathbin{\cdot}H}.
}
